 2426, 1396, 12204, 4821, 12655, 13, 1921, 264, 13852, 1496, 12692, 13775, 3575, 2493, 599, 220, 16, 1915, 64456, 791, 2018, 314, 4779, 32288, 13, 733, 421, 1105, 11, 279, 21966, 628, 1132, 958, 883, 220, 16, 4, 314, 1141, 12204, 2279, 11, 1521, 4779, 628, 7892, 1132, 220, 16, 1915, 64456, 791, 2018, 13, 561, 28736, 369, 220, 16, 15, 15, 2942, 10281, 1056, 279, 65949, 5817, 628, 8418, 13565, 13, 271, 12402, 3739, 513, 524, 421, 13818, 11, 694, 279, 4374, 369, 279, 1788, 13, 4188, 944, 31600, 1144, 9780, 314, 795, 11, 1680, 430, 1496, 60815, 28876, 11, 17754, 43900, 11, 466, 3349, 44937, 13, 24235, 1144, 2599, 795, 321, 777, 6673, 13, 1368, 264, 1746, 369, 4779, 48184, 11, 7594, 777, 12204, 34523, 1189, 524, 1438, 1691, 13, 561, 84810, 369, 279, 795, 13254, 11, 524, 279, 7853, 13, 271, 4422, 382, 3372, 440, 411], "content": "We need to answer user: \"Explain to a curious high-school student, in about 400 words, why a graphics card can be limited by memory bandwidth rather than by how fast it computes. Use one concrete analogy, give a simple worked example with made-up but plausible numbers, and finish by explaining what engineers do about it.\"\n\nNeed produce final about 400 words. Need likely exactly around 400? \"about 400 words\". Need ensure no preamble. Need one concrete analogy, simple worked example with made-up plausible numbers, finish explaining engineers do about it.\n\nLet's draft ~400 words. Need count. Let's craft.\n\nDraft:\n\"Imagine a graphics card is a kitchen. The GPU is the chef, and memory is the pantry. The chef can chop, mix, and fry very fast, but if the pantry door is narrow, only a few ingredients can be carried in at a time. The chef may stand idle, waiting for the next batch of flour, eggs, or oil. That waiting is memory bandwidth: the rate at which data can move between memory and the GPU.\n\nA GPU does not just compute one number at a time. It computes millions of tiny operations, such as shading pixels, checking textures, or updating physics. Many of those operations need data from memory. If the data arrives too slowly, the fast compute units cannot stay busy. This is called being memory-bound.\n\nHere is a simple example. Suppose a GPU can do 100 trillion floating-point operations per second, or 100 TFLOPS. Imagine each operation needs, on average, 1 byte of data from memory. Then the GPU would need 100 terabytes per second of memory bandwidth to keep every compute unit busy. But a typical high-end graphics card might have 1 terabyte per second of memory bandwidth. In that case, the GPU can only use about 1% of its compute power, because memory can supply only 1 terabyte per second. The chef is 100 times faster than the pantry door can serve ingredients.\n\nReal games are not that extreme, but the idea is the same. Some workloads need lots of data, such as high-resolution textures, ray tracing, or large simulations. Others need less data and more math. If a game is memory-bound, adding more compute cores may not help much. The bottleneck is the data pipe, not the brain.\n\nEngineers deal with this", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0007914455491118133], [760, -7.925243377685547]], [[1144, -1.9431303371675313e-05], [1902, -11.529916763305664]], [[310, -0.7010459303855896], [4087, -0.7361431121826172]], [[4087, -0.7446848750114441], [5707, -0.7792879939079285]], [[1156, -0.020105864852666855], [303, -3.9391369819641113]], [[25, -0.4151095449924469], [579, -1.2030867338180542]], [[328, -0.5870668888092041], [10033, -1.1719152927398682]], [[814, -6.723630212945864e-05], [2523, -10.120854377746582]], [[20139, -6.7949526965094265e-06], [10083, -12.447880744934082]], [[310, -0.00010574422776699066], [2423, -9.307682991027832]], [[264, -0.0001318541617365554], [21520, -9.181268692016602]], [[21520, -0.00032251313677988946], [2754, -9.361610412597656]], [[1496, -1.2517053619376384e-05], [4962, -12.885056495666504]], [[33541, -0.00015975321002770215], [54087, -9.654942512512207]], [[5286, -3.302151890238747e-05], [26